            <div xml:id="d402">

                <pb facs="1052" n="1001" xml:id="pg1001"/>

                <head>402. Memorandum From Vice President Mondale to the President’s Assistant for
                    National Security Affairs (Brzezinski)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d402-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1979-12-31">December 31, 1979</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I have been noticing with growing concern the intelligence reports of Cuban
                    covert activities including the supplying of arms to the radicals in El Salvador
                    leading to the destabilization of that government. I don’t believe it’s enough
                    to simply disclose Cuban activities there, although I think that’s important.
                    And I would hope that we could assemble immediately a small staff to consider
                    what activities, both in the intelligence collection and perhaps even in the
                    covert field, we might pursue to help defend the El Salvadorian government. I
                    have always opposed American covert activity to destabilize another government,
                    but it seems to me this is the other side of the coin, where it’s not just
                    enough to use words in the defense of a legitimate government which is being
                    destabilized, in this case by the Cubans, and I’m sure with the help of the
                    Russians. I’m not sure, nor am I recommending that we take any steps, but I
                    think it ought to be thoroughly explored and quickly.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
